3. GOVERNING LEGAL ACTS AND STATUTORY PROSECUTION:
   Any unauthorized copying, distribution, resale, or exploitation constitutes 
   willful copyright infringement, intellectual property theft, and cyber crime. 
   Strict civil litigation and criminal prosecution will be initiated under the 
   following statutory legal frameworks:

   a) The Indian Copyright Act, 1957 (and Copyright Amendment Act, 2012):
      - Section 51 & Section 63: Copyright infringement in computer programs, 
        source code, and software systems, punishable with mandatory imprisonment 
        up to three (3) years and heavy statutory financial penalties.
      - Section 63B: Knowing use and distribution of infringing copies of computer programs.

   b) The Information Technology Act, 2000 (IT Act, India):
      - Section 43: Civil penalty, damages, and compensation for unauthorized copying, 
        downloading, extraction, or possession of computer data, code, and databases.
      - Section 65: Tampering with computer source documents, punishable with imprisonment 
        up to three (3) years and/or fine.
      - Section 66: Computer-related offenses, hacking, and fraudulent misappropriation 
        of electronic property.

   c) Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (BNS) / Indian Penal Code (IPC):
      - Provisions governing Criminal Breach of Trust, Cheating, Dishonest Misappropriation 
        of Property, and Commercial Fraud.

   d) International Copyright Treaties & Global Protection:
      - Berne Convention for the Protection of Literary and Artistic Works (enforcing 
        automatic international copyright reciprocity across 180+ member nations).
      - Universal Copyright Convention and TRIPS Agreement (Trade-Related Aspects of 
        Intellectual Property Rights).
      - Digital Millennium Copyright Act (DMCA, 17 U.S.C. § 512 / § 1201).

